    written: [
      { q: "Write the percentage by volume of nitrogen and of oxygen in the atmosphere.", a: "Nitrogen: 78.08%; oxygen: 20.95%.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Which layer separates the troposphere and the stratosphere?", a: "The tropopause, whose temperature is about −80°C over the equator and −45°C over the poles.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Why is the troposphere the most important layer for life and weather?", a: "1. It is the lowest layer, with dust particles and almost all the water vapour, so clouds, rain and storms occur here.\n2. All changes in weather and climate take place in this layer.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "State the role of water vapour in the atmosphere.", a: "1. It absorbs part of insolation and holds the heat radiated by the earth like a blanket.\n2. It influences the stability and instability of air and is the source of clouds and precipitation.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Describe the importance of ozone and carbon dioxide in the atmosphere.", a: "1. Ozone, found mainly between 10 and 50 km, absorbs ultraviolet rays from the sun and acts as a filter that protects life.\n2. Carbon dioxide is transparent to incoming solar radiation but opaque to outgoing terrestrial radiation, causing the greenhouse effect.\n3. Its volume has been rising mainly because of burning of fossil fuels.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe the dust particles of the atmosphere and their significance.", a: "1. Dust includes sea salt, fine soil, smoke, soot, ash, pollen and fragments of meteors, mostly in the lower layers.\n2. They are more common in subtropical and temperate regions with dry winds than in equatorial and polar regions.\n3. They act as hygroscopic nuclei for condensation, forming clouds.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe the stratosphere and the mesosphere.", a: "1. The stratosphere extends from the tropopause to about 50 km and contains the ozone layer, which absorbs ultraviolet rays.\n2. The mesosphere lies from 50 to 80 km; its upper limit is the mesopause.\n3. In the mesosphere temperature falls with height, to about −100°C at 80 km.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe the structure of the atmosphere layer by layer, from the surface upwards, as you would label it on a vertical diagram.", a: "1. Troposphere: the lowest layer, average 13 km (8 km at poles, 18 km at the equator); weather occurs here and temperature falls about 1°C per 165 m.\n2. Tropopause separates it from the stratosphere (about −80°C over the equator, −45°C over the poles).\n3. Stratosphere: up to 50 km with the ozone layer.\n4. Mesosphere: 50–80 km, temperature falls to about −100°C.\n5. Ionosphere: 80–400 km, with ions that reflect radio waves, where temperature rises with height; above it is the exosphere, the uppermost layer.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Explain the composition of the atmosphere, covering the gases, their variation with height, water vapour and dust.", a: "1. The atmosphere is held by gravity and 99% of its mass lies within 32 km.\n2. By volume nitrogen 78.08%, oxygen 20.95%, argon 0.93%, carbon dioxide 0.036%, with traces of neon, helium and others.\n3. Oxygen is almost negligible at 120 km, and carbon dioxide and water vapour are found only up to 90 km.\n4. Water vapour is up to 4% in the warm wet tropics and less than 1% in cold deserts and polar regions; it decreases with altitude and from the equator towards the poles.\n5. Dust particles are mostly in the lower layers and serve as nuclei for condensation.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Town A (300 m above sea level) records 29°C. Town B, in the hills at 2,300 m, is on the same latitude. Use a normal lapse rate of 6.5°C per 1,000 m.\n(i) Calculate the expected temperature at B.\n(ii) At the rate of 1°C per 165 m, how much height must one climb for the temperature to fall by 10°C?\n(iii) In which layer of the atmosphere does this fall occur?", a: "(i) Height difference = 2,300 − 300 = 2,000 m. Fall = 2 × 6.5 = 13°C. Temperature at B = 29 − 13 = 16°C.\n(ii) 10 × 165 = 1,650 m.\n(iii) The troposphere, where temperature decreases with height.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
